# The server

The server renders every page of the site to HTML on the way to the browser, so a reader — and a crawler — sees the page before the client boots. It is a small Effect HTTP server started from a Bun runtime edge: it renders each of the five routes once at startup, then answers whichever one a request asks for, along with the built assets. What passes to the client is the model, inlined for hydration to read.

Rendering every route matters because the site navigates by ordinary links. A nav entry carries a real `href`, so a click leaves the page and asks the server for the next one, and a reader who reloads or arrives from outside asks for a route directly. A server that answered only the landing would give every other page a not-found.

The server stays clear of the client runtime. It renders, inlines, and serves; the browser hydrates and takes over. So each thing it imports leaves the runtime out of its build — even the pre-boot capture script comes from `@athrio/foldkit-hydration/prehydration`, a deep path that skips the hydration strategy, because that strategy reaches into Foldkit's runtime and a server runs none of it.

## Standing in for the browser {Tangle}[browser-globals.ts]

Snabbdom's style module reads `window` the moment it is imported, and a server has none — reading an undeclared `window` throws. This shim declares the global and leaves it undefined, before any Foldkit module loads, so importing the view on the server no longer throws. Where a real `window` is already present it changes nothing, so the browser stays untouched.

=>

const scope = globalThis as { window?: unknown }

if (!('window' in scope)) {
  scope.window = undefined
}

## What the server draws on

The server loads the shim first, before any Foldkit module. From `effect` come `Effect`, the `FileSystem` that reads the built shell and assets, the `Option` that fills the model's empty fields and looks a path up among the rendered pages, the `Schema` that parses what the npm registry returns, and the `Context` and `Layer` that define the server and wire it. From `effect/unstable/http` come the request and the responses. `@effect/platform-bun` supplies the Bun HTTP server, its file services, and the runtime edge. Foldkit lends `renderStatic` for the render, and, through `@athrio/foldkit-ssr`, the render itself; `@athrio/foldkit-hydration/prehydration` lends the capture script the page runs before it boots.

The site's own modules name the routes and their paths. The `Route` schema lists every page the model admits, and the `pathOf` function gives each route the path it answers on — the same function the Notes overlay stamps a note with, so a rendered page and a note agree on where the reader was standing.

=>

import './browser-globals'
import { Context, Effect, FileSystem, Layer, Option, Schema as S } from 'effect'
import {
  HttpServer,
  HttpServerRequest,
  HttpServerResponse,
} from 'effect/unstable/http'
import { BunHttpServer, BunRuntime, BunServices } from '@effect/platform-bun'
import { dirname, join } from 'node:path'
import { fileURLToPath } from 'node:url'
import { renderStatic } from 'foldkit/html'
import { PREHYDRATION_CAPTURE_SCRIPT } from '@athrio/foldkit-hydration/prehydration'
import { FoldkitRender } from '@athrio/foldkit-ssr'
import { view } from './view'
import { Route, type Model } from './model'
import { pathOf, seedNotes } from './devtools'

const distDir = join(dirname(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url)), '..', 'dist')

## The page

The page starts from the built shell and gains three things: the rendered body in place of the empty `#root`, the route's own title in place of the shell's, and, in the head, the capture script and the model inlined as JSON for hydration.

The seed is the site at rest, and every page starts from it — no chapter opened, no section jumped to, the example standing at its first frame, and the version at the workspace's own until the render fetches the latest. It names the landing's route because a model must name one; each page then overrides that single field with its own.

{{ version = ${version} }}

=>

const seed: Model = {
  route: 'home',
  chapter: '',
  navHidden: false,
  drawerOpen: false,
  runtime: 'bun',
  searchOpen: false,
  caret: 0,
  searching: false,
  activeSection: '',
  player: { struck: 0, beat: 0, playing: false, output: Option.none(), full: false },
  version: '::[version]',
  query: '',
  focus: 0,
  copied: '',
  notes: seedNotes,
  seq: 3,
  notesOpen: false,
  barCollapsed: false,
  picking: false,
  noteTab: 'open',
  aimed: Option.none(),
  draft: '',
  highlight: Option.none(),
}

const inlineJson = (value: unknown): string =>
  JSON.stringify(value).replace(/</g, '\\u003c')

const withBody = (shell: string, body: string): string =>
  shell.replace('<div id="root"></div>', () => `<div id="root">${body}</div>`)

const withSeed = (shell: string, model: Model): string =>
  shell.replace(
    '</head>',
    () =>
      `<script>${PREHYDRATION_CAPTURE_SCRIPT}</script>` +
      `<script id="foldkit-model" type="application/json">${inlineJson(model)}</script></head>`,
  )

~

A page's title belongs to its route, since a crawler reads the title before it reads anything in the body, and a reader sees it in the tab. The view already names one per route, so the server writes that name over whatever the shell carries. Matching the element rather than its exact text keeps the substitution working when the shell's own title changes.

=>

const escapeText = (text: string): string =>
  text.replace(/&/g, '&amp;').replace(/</g, '&lt;')

const withTitle = (shell: string, title: string): string =>
  shell.replace(/<title>[\s\S]*?<\/title>/, () => `<title>${escapeText(title)}</title>`)

## The latest release

The pill and the title bar both name the version, and that version is whatever npm serves as the latest `@athrio/loom` release. The server reads it once, when it renders, so a new release reaches the page with the next deploy and no change to the code. The registry answers with the release's metadata, and the `Release` schema keeps the one field the page needs — the version. A registry the server cannot reach leaves the fallback in place, so the render never fails for want of the network.

=>

const NPM_LATEST = 'https://registry.npmjs.org/@athrio/loom/latest'

const Release = S.Struct({ version: S.String })

const latestVersion = (fallback: string): Effect.Effect<string> =>
  Effect.tryPromise(() =>
    fetch(NPM_LATEST)
      .then((response) => response.json())
      .then((body) => S.decodeUnknownSync(Release)(body).version),
  ).pipe(Effect.catchCause(() => Effect.succeed(fallback)))

## Rendering one page

One model renders one page. The view turns the model into a document — a title and a body — `renderStatic` and `FoldkitRender` render that body to HTML, and the three substitutions fold the body, the title, and the model back into the shell.

=>

const pageOf = (
  render: FoldkitRender['Service'],
  shell: string,
  model: Model,
): Effect.Effect<string> =>
  Effect.gen(function* () {
    const document = view(model)
    const body = yield* render.renderToString(renderStatic(() => document.body))
    return withSeed(withTitle(withBody(shell, body), document.title), model)
  })

## Rendering the whole site once

The site is rendered at startup and held, so no request pays for a render. It reads the built shell, asks npm for the latest release once, then renders a page for every route the model admits and keys each by the path it answers on.

`Route.literals` is that list, taken from the schema rather than repeated here, so a route added to the model gains a rendered page with no change to the server. `RenderedSite` is the result: the site, rendered, one page per path.

=>

export class RenderedSite extends Context.Service<RenderedSite>()('RenderedSite', {
  make: Effect.gen(function* () {
    const fs = yield* FileSystem.FileSystem
    const render = yield* FoldkitRender
    const shell = yield* fs.readFileString(join(distDir, 'index.html'))
    const version = yield* latestVersion(seed.version)
    const pages = yield* Effect.forEach(Route.literals, (route) =>
      Effect.map(
        pageOf(render, shell, { ...seed, route, version }),
        (page) => [pathOf(route), page] as const,
      ),
    )
    return { pages: new Map(pages) } as const
  }),
}) {
  static readonly layer = Layer.effect(this, this.make).pipe(
    Layer.provide(FoldkitRender.layer),
    Layer.provide(BunServices.layer),
  )
}

## Answering a request

The server looks the request path up among the rendered pages. A path that names one returns it; anything else is a file under `dist` — the client bundle and its assets — with a plain not-found when there is none.

=>

const notFound = Effect.succeed(
  HttpServerResponse.text('Not found', { status: 404 }),
)

const asset = (pathname: string) =>
  HttpServerResponse.file(join(distDir, pathname)).pipe(
    Effect.catchCause(() => notFound),
  )

const handle = (site: RenderedSite['Service'], pathname: string) =>
  Option.match(Option.fromNullishOr(site.pages.get(pathname)), {
    onSome: (page) =>
      Effect.succeed(HttpServerResponse.text(page, { contentType: 'text/html' })),
    onNone: () => asset(pathname),
  })

## Serving it {Tangle}[server.ts]

The handler reads the request path and answers it. The server serves that handler over a Bun HTTP layer and launches from the Bun runtime edge.

=>

::[What the server draws on]

::[The page]

::[The latest release]

::[Rendering one page]

::[Rendering the whole site once]

::[Answering a request]

const app = Effect.gen(function* () {
  const request = yield* HttpServerRequest.HttpServerRequest
  const site = yield* RenderedSite
  const pathname = new URL(request.url, 'http://localhost').pathname
  return yield* handle(site, pathname)
})

const port = Number(process.env.PORT ?? 5199)

const server = HttpServer.serve(app).pipe(
  HttpServer.withLogAddress,
  Layer.provide(RenderedSite.layer),
  Layer.provide(BunHttpServer.layer({ port })),
)

BunRuntime.runMain(Layer.launch(server))
